commit 31a792aff9d21394c940412d4029b85eb6c5cd6a
Author: claude (dev113 6465e658-44ed-4e80-93cf-c9586d8683d6) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Tue Jul 28 12:06:56 2026 -0400

    skill: correct the build sequence and target descriptions

    Found by an AI-review pass over the docs; each claim re-verified against the
    engine before changing.

    - 'deb-pkg' does NOT need dist/debdist/debdsc run first. make-helper.bsh:75-91
      intercepts it and runs deb-cleanup, dist, debdist, debdsc, then deb-pkg-build
      per architecture. deb-cleanup DELETES those artifacts first, so running them by
      hand is wasted work. The "did you run genmkfile dist" error belongs to
      deb-pkg-build.
    - 'distclean' does not remove tarballs; it is currently the same as 'clean'
      (make_distclean -> make_clean; the engine's own help says so).
    - 'deb-install' installs the already-built .deb and runs installcheck; build-deps
      are deb-build-dep / deb-all-dep.
    - Build outputs go to $DISTDIR, which is '..' by default but is reassigned to
      make_cowbuilder_dist_folder under cowbuilder. genmkfile hand-rolls tar BECAUSE
      dpkg-source can only target '../'.
    - 'deb-cleanup' also runs make_clean and debian/rules clean, and deletes that
      package's artifacts from $DISTDIR -- which under cowbuilder is the .deb cache,
      so every deb-pkg wipes that package's cached files before rebuilding and a
      failed build leaves none. It executes code from the package tree, so it is not
      a pure file-removal step.
    - Override files are also read from ./debian/ and are SOURCED into the engine.
    - GENMKFILE_PATH detection is relative to the wrapper, and has a third branch.

    Generated with assistance from Claude Code.

