commit dc70f2f944af44f50295d98ffc149c654f9850fb
Author: claude (derivative-maker3 ffb70f1b-ad5f-4fe1-9c66-bbc9e65a090f) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Thu Jul 2 18:35:58 2026 -0400

    make-helper-one: fail loud when --safe-links drops a source symlink

    In install mode the per-fso loop runs 'stat -c "%a" -- "$file_with_destdir"'
    unconditionally. rsync is invoked with '--safe-links', which skips symlinks
    pointing outside the copied tree (absolute, or via '..'), so such a symlink is
    silently NOT installed and (on a clean DESTDIR) has no counterpart. 'stat'
    follows symlinks, so it then fails on the missing path and, because the
    make-helper.bsh caller runs under 'set -o errexit', aborts the whole install
    with a cryptic 'cannot statx'.

    For a symlink source, decide from the link TARGETS, not from destination
    existence/type: on a reinstall/upgrade DESTDIR may already hold a stale file OR
    a stale symlink at the same path, either of which would mask the drop. rsync
    copies a kept symlink verbatim, so the destination must be a symlink with the
    identical target; anything else (absent, a non-symlink, or a differently-aimed
    stale symlink) means rsync dropped it -> 'die' with a clear, actionable message.
    A kept-but-dangling in-tree symlink (identical target, target absent) is skipped
    rather than errored, since chmod-ing it is meaningless. A non-symlink source
    whose destination is somehow missing still falls through to 'stat' and aborts
    under errexit - also loud.

    Verified against the dist-ai genmkfile-tests install_safe_links suite: normal
    file+dir install; escaping symlink over a clean dest, a stale regular file, and
    a stale symlink all fail loud; a safe in-tree symlink installs; a dangling
    in-tree symlink is tolerated.

    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013KGErLCzVcqd2kYV2DJT5E

