commit d59b199809e509d87033bd70f2cb3b87db1c2cdf
Author: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Wed May 27 22:45:23 2026 +0000

    git-tag-push: drop unsupported '--' from 'git rev-parse --verify'

    'git rev-parse' does not accept '--' as an end-of-options separator
    (the in-file note at line 1859 already calls this out). With '--'
    present the command fails:

        $ git rev-parse --verify -- 'refs/tags/X^{commit}'
        fatal: Needed a single revision
        rc=128

    In git_tag_push_to_remote (lines 2007, 2034) this made tag_sha empty,
    so git_tag_push_cache_write emitted lines with a trailing colon and no
    SHA:

        git@github.com:org-ai-assisted/debug-misc.git:5.8-1:

    The cache 'self-healed' (both reads and writes used the same empty SHA)
    so failures were silent and re-pushes were skipped against an effectively
    unbound tag. errexit didn't catch it because make_function_run wraps the
    whole call chain in 'fn || rc=$?', suspending errexit through the
    backgrounded git_tag_push_to_remote sub-shell.

    The same broken '--' was also at line 1827 in make_git_tag_sign's
    'tag already exists at HEAD - no-op' short-circuit; that check silently
    never fires.

    Removing '--' is safe here: the arguments are always 'refs/tags/...'
    and never start with '-'. Existing stale cache lines with empty SHAs
    won't match the new full-SHA lookup, so the next run pushes once,
    appends a correct line, and the stale entry stays harmless until
    manually cleaned.

