commit d6eb1e47f970efcb4cb8fcc7a456c175f23a5f2b
Author: claude-code <claude-code@assisted-by-ai>
Date:   Wed May 6 22:20:36 2026 +0000

    make-helper-one.bsh: CI=true workaround for tree-mode-stripped exec bits

    Some upstream push paths set every new git tree entry to mode 100644
    with no way to opt out -- notably the GitHub Contents API used by
    github-mcp-server's push_files and create_or_update_file. When that
    hits an executable script in usr/bin/, the file lands in the tree at
    100644, 'test -x' returns false in make_helper, and rsync + the
    existing chmod-by-source-mode logic installs it as 0644. Any CI step
    that invokes the binary from PATH then fails with rc=126 (EACCES).

    Same bug fixed before in upstream consumers via per-repo
    'git update-index --chmod=+x' + push (e.g. developer-meta-files
    commit 63d8941). That fix-up only works from a developer's local
    checkout, so PRs pushed entirely through the MCP cannot recover.

    Add a CI-only safety net here: under CI=true (set by GitHub Actions
    and equivalent), force chmod 755 for any file installed under one of
    the conventional executable locations regardless of source-tree mode:

      /usr/bin/*
      /usr/sbin/*
      /usr/libexec/<source-pkg-name>/*

    False positives (a sourced lib gaining an exec bit it does not need)
    are functionally harmless: bash 'source' does not consult the bit.
    The behavior is gated on CI=true so production installs continue to
    mirror source mode exactly. Hard opt-out: GENMKFILE_CI_FORCE_EXEC=0.

commit caacabcd4da8b223ae2a4881415626fcf7f8b2dc
Merge: f65597b 152849b
Author: Patrick Schleizer <adrelanos@whonix.org>
Date:   Wed May 6 09:42:23 2026 -0400

